WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.847 --> 00:00:04.214
In hall B the power comes through the wall into a cabinet.

2
00:00:05.045 --> 00:00:08.133
Then transformer and switchboard feed line 3.

3
00:00:09.093 --> 00:00:11.032
The cabinet is part of switchgear.

4
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.231
It switches, protects and isolates parts of the grid.

5
00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:18.065
Four topics follow the cabinet.

6
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.283
First the devices and the installation they form together.

7
00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:27.233
Then what protects when a fault happens and how you switch safely.

8
00:00:28.507 --> 00:00:30.783
The first topic is Switching devices.

9
00:00:31.493 --> 00:00:35.382
For every device you ask what it can switch on and what it can break.

10
00:00:36.431 --> 00:00:40.506
A short circuit hits much harder than the normal current to line 3.

11
00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:43.987
So breaking it is a heavier job than switching on.

12
00:00:44.968 --> 00:00:49.183
So you look at the difference between a circuit breaker and a load-break switch.

13
00:00:50.496 --> 00:00:54.281
The second topic is MV installations and stations.

14
00:00:55.042 --> 00:00:59.733
An installation is a row of steel cabinets, and each cabinet is a panel.

15
00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.770
With air between the parts an installation is big.

16
00:01:04.509 --> 00:01:07.806
With gas in a sealed tank it becomes much more compact.

17
00:01:08.834 --> 00:01:14.465
In a secondary substation the MV part is usually an RMU, one compact block.

18
00:01:15.818 --> 00:01:18.779
The third topic is Protection and short circuits.

19
00:01:19.468 --> 00:01:22.254
Suppose a short circuit happens at line 3.

20
00:01:23.004 --> 00:01:25.012
Then the current must stop fast.

21
00:01:26.091 --> 00:01:29.539
A protection measures the current and compares it with a setting.

22
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:32.094
At a fault the breaker opens.

23
00:01:33.108 --> 00:01:36.603
A wrong setting makes the breaker open too often, or never.

24
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.675
So the installer tests the protection relay and records the setting.

25
00:01:42.975 --> 00:01:45.100
The fourth topic is Switching safely.

26
00:01:45.822 --> 00:01:50.478
Work on the cabinet is mostly done dead, so switched off and made safe.

27
00:01:51.595 --> 00:01:55.089
The five safety rules apply, always in the same order.

28
00:01:55.803 --> 00:01:57.812
Each rule stops a real failure.

29
00:01:58.831 --> 00:02:01.803
Only an appointed installer works on the cabinet.

30
00:02:02.563 --> 00:02:04.397
You go along and touch nothing.

31
00:02:05.523 --> 00:02:09.935
The installer tests with a voltage detector and places an earthing set.

32
00:02:11.065 --> 00:02:13.979
Then the installer works safely on the cabinet.

33
00:02:15.317 --> 00:02:16.106
The whole map.

34
00:02:16.834 --> 00:02:20.979
Switchgear and protection is the second station of Energy and the grid.

35
00:02:22.006 --> 00:02:23.736
Every topic has four levels.

36
00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:28.053
Start with the lesson on circuit breakers at Basics level.

37
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:30.933
It shows what each device can break.

38
00:02:32.005 --> 00:02:34.420
At Working knowledge level come the nameplate,

39
00:02:34.582 --> 00:02:38.088
distribution automation, selectivity and proving dead.

40
00:02:39.113 --> 00:02:41.470
At Advanced level come putting out the arc,

41
00:02:41.621 --> 00:02:45.742
busbars, calculating short-circuit current and who may switch.

42
00:02:46.778 --> 00:02:49.808
At Expert level it is about too high a fault level,

43
00:02:49.912 --> 00:02:54.080
SF6, solar parks and the customer’s own MV installation.

44
00:02:55.096 --> 00:02:59.276
Every lesson and every film in this module has a place on this map.
